1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,156
Priya's answer was no before she had even finished the question.

2
00:00:04,156 --> 00:00:05,286
Every manager's is.

3
00:00:05,286 --> 00:00:10,044
You would give a new hire a desk, a login for one system, and a first task small

4
00:00:10,044 --> 00:00:10,996
enough to check.

5
00:00:10,996 --> 00:00:15,575
Nobody hands over the keys on day one, and nobody thinks that is being overly

6
00:00:15,575 --> 00:00:16,111
cautious.

7
00:00:16,111 --> 00:00:18,252
It is just how you bring someone on.

8
00:00:18,252 --> 00:00:21,047
Software has trained us to expect the opposite.

9
00:00:21,047 --> 00:00:25,507
You install an app, tick a permissions box without reading it, and it works

10
00:00:25,507 --> 00:00:27,054
immediately at full power.

11
00:00:27,054 --> 00:00:30,027
That habit is the one this course exists to break.

12
00:00:30,027 --> 00:00:34,904
Think about the last time you watched someone new arrive anywhere you have worked.

13
00:00:34,904 --> 00:00:39,841
However good their references were, their first week had a shape: one system login,

14
00:00:39,841 --> 00:00:40,495
not twelve.

15
00:00:40,495 --> 00:00:44,658
A first task someone could check without embarrassment on either side.

16
00:00:44,658 --> 00:00:49,237
A colleague glancing over the work, not because anyone expected disaster, but

17
00:00:49,237 --> 00:00:52,628
because checking early is cheaper than apologising later.

18
00:00:52,628 --> 00:00:54,471
Nobody wrote a policy for that.

19
00:00:54,471 --> 00:00:59,051
It is simply what trusting a stranger with real work looks like when the work

20
00:00:59,051 --> 00:00:59,526
matters.

21
00:00:59,526 --> 00:01:02,441
An agent is a stranger with excellent references.

22
00:01:02,441 --> 00:01:04,106
The references are the demo.

23
00:01:04,106 --> 00:01:06,663
The stranger is what shows up on your data.

24
00:01:07,213 --> 00:01:11,690
Notice that the right hand column is not slower for the sake of caution.

25
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:16,789
It is slower on day one and faster for the rest of the year, because every failure

26
00:01:16,789 --> 00:01:21,888
it produces is small and survivable instead of the kind that ends up in an apology

27
00:01:21,888 --> 00:01:23,007
email to a client.

28
00:01:23,007 --> 00:01:24,810
Make the difference concrete.

29
00:01:24,810 --> 00:01:27,484
Two people set up the same follow up agent.

30
00:01:27,484 --> 00:01:31,401
The installer connects everything it asks for and lets it send.

31
00:01:31,401 --> 00:01:36,375
Three weeks in, it chases a client who had already replied, warmly, to close the

32
00:01:36,375 --> 00:01:41,536
matter, and the client reads the chase as nobody at the firm talking to each other.

33
00:01:41,536 --> 00:01:44,459
The onboarder set the same agent to draft only.

34
00:01:44,459 --> 00:01:49,557
The same wrong chase appears in her drafts folder, she deletes it in four seconds,

35
00:01:49,557 --> 00:01:54,283
and she has learned something specific: this agent cannot yet tell a closing

36
00:01:54,283 --> 00:01:56,211
pleasantry from an open thread.

37
00:01:56,211 --> 00:02:00,812
Same software, same mistake, and only one of them had to say sorry for it.

38
00:02:00,812 --> 00:02:03,175
The difference was never intelligence.

39
00:02:03,175 --> 00:02:05,911
It was the order in which trust was granted.

40
00:02:06,461 --> 00:02:10,942
Read that list again and notice you did not learn it from a management book.

41
00:02:10,942 --> 00:02:14,540
You already do all four for people without thinking about it.

42
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:19,434
The entire method in this section is applying the same four habits to software that

43
00:02:19,434 --> 00:02:24,329
can now read, decide and act, instead of quietly assuming they do not apply because

44
00:02:24,329 --> 00:02:26,570
the new hire happens to be a workflow.

45
00:02:26,570 --> 00:02:31,288
The first habit is the one people most want to skip, so here is why it cannot be

46
00:02:31,288 --> 00:02:33,293
skipped for an agent specifically.

47
00:02:33,293 --> 00:02:38,246
A human new starter fills the gaps in their instructions from context: they hear how

48
00:02:38,246 --> 00:02:43,082
colleagues talk to clients, they notice what gets escalated, they ask when unsure.

49
00:02:43,082 --> 00:02:45,618
An agent has none of that ambient learning.

50
00:02:45,618 --> 00:02:50,453
It has exactly what you wrote down, and it will apply what you wrote with perfect,

51
00:02:50,453 --> 00:02:54,581
literal confidence to situations you never imagined when you wrote it.

52
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:57,353
Writing the job description is not bureaucracy.

53
00:02:57,353 --> 00:03:02,247
It is the only channel through which your judgement reaches the agent at all, which

54
00:03:02,247 --> 00:03:07,142
is why it comes before anything else and why the next two lectures are spent on it.

55
00:03:07,692 --> 00:03:12,503
One reading note on this pipeline: three of the habits are stages you move through,

56
00:03:12,503 --> 00:03:15,806
but the fourth is a boundary that applies at every stage.

57
00:03:15,806 --> 00:03:20,385
The things an agent may never do alone on day ninety are the same things it may

58
00:03:20,385 --> 00:03:21,892
never do alone on day one.

59
00:03:21,892 --> 00:03:23,747
Autonomy grows inside that line.

60
00:03:23,747 --> 00:03:28,384
The line itself does not move, and drawing it before the first stage is what the

61
00:03:28,384 --> 00:03:30,007
next three lectures are for.

62
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:32,447
Is the hour genuinely worth it?

63
00:03:32,447 --> 00:03:36,046
Run the honest arithmetic, because this course promised to.

64
00:03:36,046 --> 00:03:39,705
The job description takes about half an hour the first time.

65
00:03:39,705 --> 00:03:42,633
A trial costs you checking time for a few weeks.

66
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:47,634
Against that: the follow up mistake above cost the installer a client apology and,

67
00:03:47,634 --> 00:03:50,988
more expensively, her own confidence in the whole idea.

68
00:03:50,988 --> 00:03:55,928
Most people who abandon agents do not abandon them because the technology failed.

69
00:03:55,928 --> 00:04:00,625
They abandon them because an early, avoidable mess scared them off before the

70
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:01,601
savings arrived.

71
00:04:01,601 --> 00:04:04,406
The setup hour is not overhead on the payback.

72
00:04:04,406 --> 00:04:09,102
It is what makes the payback survivable long enough to collect, and Section 9

73
00:04:09,102 --> 00:04:14,164
subtracts every minute of checking time from the savings before it ever shows you a

74
00:04:14,164 --> 00:04:14,591
number.

75
00:04:14,591 --> 00:04:19,532
One thing to do before the next lecture: name the task you would hand a competent

76
00:04:19,532 --> 00:04:21,483
new hire on their first morning.

77
00:04:21,483 --> 00:04:24,899
Not the hardest thing on your list, the checkable thing.

78
00:04:24,899 --> 00:04:29,290
That task is your first agent's job, and the next lecture writes its job

79
00:04:29,290 --> 00:04:30,022
description.

80
00:04:30,022 --> 00:04:34,779
Then we cover how trust actually gets earned, in stages, once that description

81
00:04:34,779 --> 00:04:35,206
exists.
